<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phoenice</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Foinike, Finiq</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phoenice was an ancient Greek city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaonia">Chaonia</a>, in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, near modern Finiq in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>. It was the chief town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaonians">Chaonians</a> and a frequent meeting place of the Epirote League. Illyrian raiders sent by Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teuta">Teuta</a> sacked it in 230 BCE, and in 205 BCE it gave its name to the peace that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-macedonian-war">First Macedonian War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phoenice.jpg" alt="Phoenice" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robmor01 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2nd_c._BC_shops.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The city stood high on an almost impregnable hill commanding a fertile valley. It was the wealthiest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and had the strongest walls until the Roman conquest. Its earliest walls were of massive blocks up to 3.6 metres thick, built against Corcyraeans and Illyrian raiders, and they were extended at the end of the 4th century BCE as part of the defences of Pyrrhus. Public buildings rose on the acropolis from the second half of the 5th century BCE, a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> was built in the 3rd century BCE, and the patron god was probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena-polias">Athena Polias</a>. The name, shared with at least 16 places of the Greek world, is of non-Indo-European origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the assassination of Queen Deidamia II, last of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeacidae">Aeacid dynasty</a>, in about 233 BCE, the monarchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> was abolished and Phoenice became the centre of the federal government of the Epirote League. Teuta's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a> took the town when its garrison of 800 Gaulish mercenaries surrendered it, and defeated a relieving army of the league at the Battle of Phoenice. Their murder of Roman merchants there led in time to the First Illyrian War. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-macedonian-war">Third Macedonian War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaonians">Chaonians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thesprotians">Thesprotians</a> sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from Phoenice under Charops, and the city was spared the devastation that followed the Roman conquest of 167 BCE.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> later renovated the city; its great cistern dates from the 2nd century CE. In the 5th and 6th centuries it was the see of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, with a baptistery and a basilica, and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> fortified a neighbouring hill. The city vanished after the 6th century, when the centre of the area moved to nearby Mesopotamon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Excavations began in 1924 with an Italian mission under Luigi Ugolini, whose work served the ambitions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a>, and resumed in 1958 with a joint Albanian-USSR team. Work in 1980 to 1981 uncovered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, and excavations have continued since 2000. The city is now part of an archaeological park.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Phoenice” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The earthquake of 551 CE destroyed most of the cities of <b>Phoenice</b>, and in 636 the Muslim conquest of the Levant took the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>They formed a federal state, the Koinon of the Epirotes, whose meetings were often held at <b>Phoenice</b> in Chaonia, near the Ionian coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/epirote-league">Epirote League</a></li></ul></section>
